# N4-B: the port's host-UI seam is live and this app is the thing behind it.
#
# The port calls four hooks -- Start once the window exists, PollPad while it assembles each
# frame's pad, Frame after that, Stop after the loop -- and ships weak no-ops for all four so the
# desktop executable and the unit tests still link. That makes "did the app's adapter win" a real
# question rather than a formality: a build that resolved every hook to the port's own no-op links,
# launches and presents the same frame, with no touch interface at all.
#
# So this scenario checks the seam, not the geometry. The interface's own layout is SunPad's,
# vendored byte-for-byte, and the fidelity claim about it is a diff against the sibling project
# rather than a screenshot. What cannot be settled by a diff is whether the port is calling into
# the app: the two expected lines are the adapter's, and one of them can only be printed from
# inside the port's frame loop.
#
# Both go to stderr through NSLog as well as to the overlay's own runtime log, so the driver sees
# them; the runtime log's copy is what the three-dot menu's report row would attach.
env STRIKERS_LOG_SCENES=1
require-frame 180
expect [SunPad] host ui: overlay
expect [SunPad] host ui: first pad poll
shot hostui_boot
dump hostui_boot
